       (the painted L floors below nominal — a warm-brown, never gray, never maroon). */
    --cartoon-ink-fallback: #413626;

    --shadow-cartoon:       -3px 3px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead);
    --shadow-cartoon-hover: -4px 4px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead);
    --shadow-soft:     0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
    --shadow-elevated: 0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    --shadow-modal:
        -4px 4px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead),
        0 24px 64px color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 18%, transparent);

    /* §5c CONSTELLATION — dark arm. The dark-mode lift: the node
       tones step UP off the ink ground (the light arm's mid-grey would vanish on
       L6 black), the hairline goes warm off-white, the field carries a touch more
       presence to read at all. PLAIN-hsl literals, NEVER light-dark()/var(). */
    --constellation-node:      hsl(42 9% 72%);
    --constellation-node-dim:  hsl(42 8% 56%);
    --constellation-line:      hsl(45 12% 86%);
    /* Anomaly — brightened for the deep canvas (same neutral-default register;
       the consumer still overrides to its brand). */
    --constellation-accent:    hsl(20 70% 60%);
    /* The dark hairlines lift harder over the ink ground, so the edge multipliers
       run a touch lower than light to keep the field recessive. */
    --constellation-edge-alpha:          0.20;
    --constellation-edge-focus-alpha:  0.32;
    /* §7 — the ink ground needs MORE field presence than cream to read at all, so
       the dark alpha is modestly HIGHER than the light arm — but still BELOW the
       slides 1.0 max-legibility reference (the recessive midpoint, not 1.0). */
    --constellation-alpha:               0.88;
    /* ACCENT-edge skin — dark arm. The floor stays 0 (the dark
       ground already lifts the hairlines, so no perceptual-floor lift is needed); the
       accent-incident multiplier runs a touch louder on the deeper ground so the
       flagged-node tether reads (the 0.34 dark reference). */
    --constellation-edge-floor:          0;
    --constellation-edge-accent-alpha:   0.34;

    /* The dark arm mints NO second alpha ladder. Nine hand-tuned dark opacities used
       to mirror nine light ones, and the two registers could drift apart without
       either being wrong on its own. The veil's dark arm below is one ink, one base
       and one step, and every rung follows.

       Grain, DARK arm. A multiply blend on a near-black canvas needs LESS alpha than
       on cream — the honest inversion the measurements force. */
    --paper-grain-opacity: 0.16;

    /* ── The veil's DARK arm ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The dark ladder is the ink ladder: the plate DIMS toward a warm near-black INK at
       `base ± n·step`. (The light arm is the `--card` pole at the bound,
       tokens/glass.css; its rungs are bound arithmetic, so the dark arm declares its
       own here rather than inheriting them.) The BASE is the measured dark resting
       rung; every rung is arithmetic on base and step.

       (The state veils are the exception, and they are governed elsewhere: a grasp or
       selection veil moves AWAY from the plate, which in dark means lighter. That is a
       STATE law, not the medium's.) */
    --glass-veil-ink:  oklch(0.17 0.03 70);
    --glass-veil-base: 0.18;
    --glass-veil-step: 0.04;

    --glass-veil-wash:     calc(var(--glass-veil-base) - 2 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-quiet:    calc(var(--glass-veil-base) - 1 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-resting:  var(--glass-veil-base);
    --glass-veil-floating: calc(var(--glass-veil-base) + 1 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-overlay:  calc(var(--glass-veil-base) + 2 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    /* The off-ladder footprints in steps off resting: dock −1, crown +1, dialog +1,
       sheet +2, chassis −2. */
    --glass-veil-chassis:  calc(var(--glass-veil-base) - 2 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-dock:     calc(var(--glass-veil-base) - 1 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-crown:    calc(var(--glass-veil-base) + 1 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-dialog:   calc(var(--glass-veil-base) + 1 * var(--glass-veil-step));
    --glass-veil-sheet:    calc(var(--glass-veil-base) + 2 * var(--glass-veil-step));

    /* The FOCUS-VEIL dim, DARK arm (glass/focus-veil.css; the light arm is the plain
       per-mode pair in tokens/glass.css). A plain `.dark` re-declaration, never a
       `light-dark()` fold — the dim is one leg of a per-mode pair and the fold is the
       trap that computes the whole declaration away. Dark needs LESS veil than light to
       read the same de-emphasis over an already-dark page: 40% against light's 50%. */
    --glass-focus-veil-dim: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-plate-overlay) 40%, transparent);

    /* The surface-aware FOREGROUND register, DARK arm (the N13
       whisper collapse where it bites hardest). The dark page-muted --neutral-5 (L62,
       AA 7.70:1 vs page [2026-08-05: re-derived from ~~7.64~~]) COLLAPSES over the
       lifted luminous-dark plate
       (the ~~L16~~ [2026-09-22 · O-32 CT2: L17, hsl(26 22% 17%)] card
       at the 12% --glass-veil-rung composites the bright backdrop UP — the
       measured 1.15-3.29:1). The on-glass muted rung lifts BRIGHTER (the mirror of the
       light arm's darkening) so it clears AA over the composited dark plate WHILE
       staying subordinate to the L90 --foreground ink (the quiet-subordinate hierarchy
       preserved — NOT a blanket lift to full ink, which the bright bucket already does).
       Warm-amber (the identity), NOT the slides deck hsl(45 …) literal
       (the deck is the REFERENCE; the library re-derives against its own --card). The
       light arm lives in tokens/glass.css (the plain per-mode pair — the house idiom). */
    --on-glass-muted:        hsl(34 16% 72%);   /* brighter than the collapsed --neutral-5 L62 (WCAG 7.52:1 / APCA |Lc| 61.7 vs the luminous-dark composited quiet plate; subordinate to --foreground) */
    --on-glass-muted-strong: hsl(36 13% 81%);   /* the -strong rung, one step more present. Held at L81: at L78 the BRIGHTEST on-glass mid-tone plate (the configurator preset-label glass-capsule, composited [100,85,68] L≈0.10) drives the -strong caption to WCAG 4.33 — below AA. L81 clears the whole plate set (configurator 4.68 · shadow-stage 6.89 · texture-panel 6.47 · feedback-tone 6.74 · selected-chip 5.10) while STAYING subordinate to the L90 --foreground ink (L0.634 < L0.794). One register re-point lifts all -strong consumers in lockstep — the DRY register-level fix, NOT a per-site paste. */
    --input-on-glass:        hsl(26 12% 22%);   /* the form-well fill — grounded warm-dark so the L90 input TEXT reads against a stable base (9.3:1) */
    /* (the recessed meter track's dark arm RETIRED — `--track-well-recess` is one
       derivation off `--background`, declared on `.track-well`/`.track-ground`
       (styles/track-well.css) and NOT in this token layer, so the dark host's own
       `--background` is what it reads and no arm is owed here. Re-declaring it in
       this file would re-fork the law; the seam gate counts the declaration
       across all of src/styles and holds it at ONE.) */

    /* The --surface-tint-* dark arm,
       --foreground-DERIVED. The light family is color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) N%,
       transparent) — over a near-black dark card a LIGHT-ink (dark --foreground IS light
       cream) tint composites to near-black: the 9-rung chip/hairline/divider scale is
       INVISIBLE in dark. The dark arm re-anchors the mix ink to a BRIGHTER warm off-white
       so a chip/hairline LIFTS off the deep card (the inverse intent of light's dark-ink
       tint). A hardcoded hsl(48 12% 96%) literal ×12 would sit at the
       EXACT OKLab H95.1° yellow-green — a cast, not warm
       material — and be a per-rung literal a --foreground re-point can never reach (the
       architectural asymmetry with the light arm + the substitution-vs-redeclaration trap
       at the token mint); so each rung is a oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975
       c h) RELATIVE-COLOR derivation: the recipe extracts the (now-warmed) dark --foreground's
       warm hue + chroma and lifts the lightness to 0.975 (so the chip
       lift off the ~~L16~~ [2026-09-22 · O-32 CT2: L17, hsl(26 22% 17%)] card is
       preserved) — the ink CARRIES the --foreground's warm hue
       (OKLab H75.4°) BY DERIVATION (re-point the dark ink → every rung re-resolves warm; a
       future re-tune reaches all 12 from ONE --foreground edit — the symmetry the light arm
       has had). The OUTER mix STAYS color-mix(in srgb, … N%, transparent) — the
       SAME 12-rung α ladder + the SAME in-srgb interpolation space the light arm uses (the
       fence holds — the oklch(from …) is the INK SOURCE, not the tint mix space).
       This re-resolves every --surface-tint-* consumer (chip backplates, the dock hairline
       and separator, the spine) in lockstep, zero per-site edit. The 0.975 lift
       sits well under the sRGB gamut edge (the css-relative-color book's \"browsers don't
       gamut-map yet\" caveat does not bite — at 0.975 the small high-L chroma resolves clean;
       lift→1.0 drifts the hue, so 0.975 is the calibrated pin). The css-relative-color
       chronic's FIRST live consumer (the tolerant α-mix the book was waiting for). */
    --surface-tint-4:  color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h)  4%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-6:  color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h)  6%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-8:  color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h)  8%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-10: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 10%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-12: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 12%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-15: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 15%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-18: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 18%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-22: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 22%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-25: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 25%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-40: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 40%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-70: color-mix(in srgb, oklch(from var(--foreground) 0.975 c h) 70%, transparent);

    /* Hairline-accent dark mirror — see light counterpart at line ~404. */
    --hairline-catch-light: inset 0 0.5px 0 0 rgb(255 255 255 / 0.05);
    --hairline-under-shadow: 0 0.5px 0 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06);

    /* No `--glass-edge-light` re-point here. The name is a pure indirection onto
       `--glass-material-rim`, whose legs are re-declared below — so the dark rim
       reaches it without a second per-mode write to keep in sync. */

    /* The rim's DARK leg — plain per-mode pair, NEVER a `light-dark()` round an inset
       (an inset fragment inside `light-dark()` computes the WHOLE box-shadow to `none`
       and the rim silently vanishes).

       0.08, BELOW the light arm's 0.10. This inverts the shipped dark-brighter
       doctrine, and deliberately: the old 0.40 made the rim the brightest thing on a
       near-black page, which is a bright ring, not a silhouette. A dark plate separates
       because the page behind it is dark and the plate's own ink is deeper still — the
       rim only has to say where the edge is. The under-shadow holds warm at 6%/4% and
       does the grounding in both modes. The key sign tokens are inherited from :root,
       not re-declared, so the lit edge agrees with the cartoon cast in both modes. Both
       legs carry the `--glass-level` term for the same reason the light arm does: the
       rim is the glass's edge, and the a11y brackets flatten the material through that
       ONE knob rather than through a second list of tokens. */
    --glass-rim-top: inset 0 var(--glass-key-lit-y) 0
        hsl(0 0% 100% / calc(0.08 * var(--glass-level)));
    --glass-rim-bottom:
        inset 0 var(--glass-key-shade-y) 0
            color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) calc(6% * var(--glass-level)), transparent),
        inset var(--glass-key-shade-x) 0 0
            color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) calc(4% * var(--glass-level)), transparent);

    /* The `.glass-capsule-track` recess ink, DARK arm (the
       PLAIN per-mode pair's dark leg; the light arm is in `tokens/glass-fx.css`).
       NEVER a `light-dark()` fragment (the inset-shadow trap). Deeper on the dark
       canvas (11%) so the sunken well still reads against the near-black page. */
    --tab-track-recess-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 11%, transparent);

    /* The capsule's warm floor, DARK peer. The light arm's `oklch(0.88 0.1 75)` is a
       near-white cream: painted on a lozenge over a near-black page it is a lamp, not a
       warm floor, and the fill's ΔL against the field runs far past the light arm's.
       The dark peer keeps the HUE and the chroma that make the floor a floor, at a
       lightness that belongs on the deep canvas. */
    --glass-capsule-warm: oklch(0.42 0.06 75);

    /* The curvature sheen is genuinely SOFTER on the deep canvas (the
       prior dark arm re-declared the overlay BYTE-IDENTICAL to light, so it read
       HARDER over the dark substrate — an unsoftened-dark defect). The `:root`
       `--glass-curvature-overlay` already reads `var(--glass-curvature-intensity)`,
       so lowering just the intensity here re-resolves the SAME warm-cream recipe at
       a softer alpha — no byte-identical re-declaration. */
    --glass-curvature-intensity: 0.012;

    /* Gold — boosted for dark (oklch) */
    --gold: oklch(0.784 0.143 86.0);
    --gold-light: oklch(0.898 0.182 97.4);
    --gold-dark: oklch(0.678 0.150 63.8);

    /* Silver — lifted for dark (mirrors gold's dark
       arm: the polished metal lifts ~+0.03 L so the structure register reads on
       the deep canvas. `--silver-deep` is mode-invariant — NOT re-declared here,
       exactly as `--gold-deep` is omitted from this dark block). The cool hue
       holds; the chroma stays near-achromatic. */
    --silver: oklch(0.792 0.012 255);
    --silver-light: oklch(0.912 0.010 255);
    --silver-dark: oklch(0.648 0.014 255);

    /* Bronze — lifted for dark (mirrors gold's dark arm: the
       warm metal lifts ~+0.03 L so the bronze register reads on the deep canvas.
       `--bronze-deep` is mode-invariant — NOT re-declared here, exactly as
       `--gold-deep`/`--silver-deep` are omitted from this dark block). The warm hue
       holds; the chroma stays above the floor. The `.dark`-class fallback
       for engines without `light-dark()` (the §2c lockstep — the light-dark arm AND
       this dark fallback move TOGETHER). */
    --bronze: oklch(0.650 0.090 54.0);
    --bronze-light: oklch(0.790 0.110 60.0);
    --bronze-dark: oklch(0.560 0.085 50.0);
}
